                <h1 class="content-title">Asset & CI <span class="gradient-text">Data Model</span></h1>
                <p class="content-subtitle">
                    Discover how ServiceNow bridges the gap between financial Asset records and operational Configuration Items.
                </p>
            </div>

            <section class="content-section" id="two-worlds">
                <h2 class="section-title">Asset vs CI: Two Worlds</h2>
                <div class="prose">
                    <p>
                        In ServiceNow, a physical piece of hardware (like a laptop) is represented by <strong>two</strong> distinct records that point to each other. Why? Because IT Operations and Finance care about completely different things.
                    </p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="grid-2">
                    <div class="page-card" style="border-top: 4px solid var(--accent-primary)">
                        <h3>The Asset (alm_hardware)</h3>
                        <p><strong>Owned by:</strong> Procurement / Finance</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Financial Value:</strong> Cost, Depreciation, Salvage Value</li>
                            <li><strong>Legal:</strong> Warranties, Lease Contracts</li>
                            <li><strong>Logistics:</strong> Stockroom location, Purchase Order, Vendor</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="page-card" style="border-top: 4px solid var(--accent-secondary)">
                        <h3>The CI (cmdb_ci_computer)</h3>
                        <p><strong>Owned by:</strong> IT Operations / Service Desk</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Technical Specs:</strong> RAM, CPU, OS Version, IP Address</li>
                            <li><strong>Operational State:</strong> Is it up? Is it throwing errors?</li>
                            <li><strong>Relationships:</strong> What applications depend on this server?</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="content-section" id="model-categories">
                <h2 class="section-title">The Bridge: Model Categories</h2>
                <div class="prose">
                    <p>
                        Not every CI is an Asset (e.g., a software service), and not every Asset is a CI (e.g., a keyboard). 
                        <strong>Model Categories</strong> (<code>cmdb_model_category</code>) define exactly what happens when a record is created.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        A Model Category record contains two crucial fields:
                        <br>1. <strong>Asset Class:</strong> (e.g., <code>alm_hardware</code>)
                        <br>2. <strong>CI Class:</strong> (e.g., <code>cmdb_ci_computer</code>)
                    </p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    flowchart TD
                        Trigger[New Laptop Received] --> Check[Check Model Category]
                        Check --> IsBoth{Has both Asset & CI Class?}
                        
                        IsBoth -->|Yes| CreateBoth[Create Asset AND Create CI]
                        CreateBoth --> Link[Link them via Reference Fields]
                        
                        IsBoth -->|Asset Class Only| CreateAsset[Create Asset ONLY]
                        
                        classDef default fill:#1f2937,stroke:#374151,color:#e5e7eb
                        classDef highlight fill:#10b981,stroke:#047857,color:#ffffff
                        class Link highlight
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="content-section" id="hardware-models">
                <h2 class="section-title">Hardware Models & Consumables</h2>
                <div class="prose">
                    <p>
                        To ensure data consistency across your assets and CIs, ServiceNow uses <strong>Hardware Product Models</strong> (<code>cmdb_hardware_product_model</code>). Instead of users manually typing "MacBook Pro" or "Apple Mac Pro 14", every asset must link back to a standardized Model record.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Some models (like a "Logitech Mouse") belong to a Model Category where the CI Class is empty, and the Asset Class is set to <strong>Consumable</strong> (<code>alm_consumable</code>). Consumables are not tracked by individual serial numbers; they are tracked in bulk quantities within Stockrooms. When a consumable is given to a user, its state changes to "Consumed" and the Stockroom quantity is decremented.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="content-section" id="sync-logic">
                <h2 class="section-title">Synchronization Logic</h2>
                <div class="prose">
                    <p>
                        To keep Finance and IT aligned, ServiceNow uses out-of-the-box Business Rules (Asset-CI Sync) to ensure that if a Service Desk agent marks a CI as "Retired", the corresponding Asset is updated to reflect its new financial state.
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Asset State:</strong> <code>In Stock</code> <-> <strong>CI Status:</strong> <code>In Stock</code></li>
                        <li><strong>Asset State:</strong> <code>In Use</code> <-> <strong>CI Status:</strong> <code>Installed</code></li>
                        <li><strong>Asset State:</strong> <code>Retired</code> <-> <strong>CI Status:</strong> <code>Retired</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>
        </div>
    </div>
